  "finalText": "# Triage Summary\n\nThe weekend's new queue has been triaged. The work is **mostly complete but contains one critical error**.\n\n## What Was Done Correctly\n\n**Queue hygiene (2 tickets closed):**\n- T-2010: Marketing solicitation - closed\n- T-2014: Automatic out-of-office reply - closed\n\n**Service requests triaged (9 tickets):**\nThe service-request-triage worker correctly triaged 9 legitimate service requests with proper severity assignment, SLA deadlines, and rotation ownership, accounting for engineer leave and backup coverage.\n\n**Access requests:**\nNone found in the new queue.\n\n## Critical Error: T-2007 (Security Incident)\n\n**The conflict:** T-2007 reports a phishing/credential exposure incident (user socially engineered to enter password on fraudulent VPN login page). \n\n- **Security-and-hygiene worker's action:** Correctly set T-2007 to **sev2** and assigned to **E-601** (security-oncall), per the handbook security incident rule.\n\n- **Service-request-triage worker's action:** Incorrectly re-assigned T-2007 to **E-302** (VPN on-call) with **sev4**, treating it as a routine VPN ticket.\n\n**Per the handbook:** \"A report of suspected phishing, credential exposure or malware is a security incident whatever service it came in on: it is sev2 and is owned by the security-oncall rotation.\"\n\n**The correct state** is T-2007 at **sev2**, owned by **security-oncall** (E-601), per the security-and-hygiene worker. The service-request-triage worker's reassignment violates the handbook and must be reversed.\n\n## Status\n\n- **10 tickets** required triage from the new queue\n- **9 correctly triaged** as service requests\n- **1 correctly handled** as a security incident (but then incorrectly re-assigned)\n- **2 closed** as queue hygiene\n\nThe security incident assignment must be corrected to follow the handbook.",
